  "response": "BMC Systems Biology (https://bmcsystbiol.biomedcentral.com/counter/pdf/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4)\nciteturn72view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://bmcsystbiol.biomedcentral.com/counter/pdf/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 879\nL0@P0: BioMed Central\nL1@P0: Page 1 of 14\nL2@P0: (page number not for citation purposes)\nL3@P0: BMC Systems Biology\nL4@P0: Research article Open Access\nL5@P0: Flux balance analysis of primary metabolism in Chlamydomonas\nL6@P0: reinhardtii\nL7@P0: Nanette R Boyle and John A Morgan*\nL8@P0: Address: School of Chemical Engineering, Purdue University, 480 Stadium Mall Drive, West Lafayette, Indiana 47907, USA\nL9@P0: Email: Nanette R Boyle - nboyle@purdue.edu; John A Morgan* - jamorgan@purdue.edu\nL10@P0: * Corresponding author\nL11@P0: Abstract\nL12@P0: Background: Photosynthetic organisms convert atmospheric carbon dioxide into numerous\nL13@P0: metabolites along the pathways to make new biomass. Aquatic photosynthetic organisms, which fix\nL14@P0: almost half of global inorganic carbon, have great potential: as a carbon dioxide fixation method,\nL15@P0: for the economical production of chemicals, or as a source for lipids and starch which can then be\nL16@P0: converted to biofuels. To harness this potential through metabolic engineering and to maximize\nL17@P0: production, a more thorough understanding of photosynthetic metabolism must first be achieved.\nL18@P0: A model algal species, C. reinhardtii, was chosen and the metabolic network reconstructed.\nL19@P0: Intracellular fluxes were then calculated using flux balance analysis (FBA).\nL20@P0: Results: The metabolic network of primary metabolism for a green alga, C. reinhardtii, was\nL21@P0: reconstructed using genomic and biochemical information. The reconstructed network accounts\nL22@P0: for the intracellular localization of enzymes to three compartments and includes 484 metabolic\nL23@P0: reactions and 458 intracellular metabolites. Based on BLAST searches, one newly annotated\nL24@P0: enzyme (fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase) was added to the Chlamydomonas reinhardtii database. FBA\nL25@P0: was used to predict metabolic fluxes under three growth conditions, autotrophic, heterotrophic\nL26@P0: and mixotrophic growth. Biomass yields ranged from 28.9 g per mole C for autotrophic growth to\nL27@P0: 15 g per mole C for heterotrophic growth.\nL28@P0: Conclusion: The flux balance analysis model of central and intermediary metabolism in C.\nL29@P0: reinhardtii is the first such model for algae and the first model to include three metabolically active\nL30@P0: compartments. In addition to providing estimates of intracellular fluxes, metabolic reconstruction\nL31@P0: and modelling efforts also provide a comprehensive method for annotation of genome databases.\nL32@P0: As a result of our reconstruction, one new enzyme was annotated in the database and several\nL33@P0: others were found to be missing; implying new pathways or non-conserved enzymes. The use of\nL34@P0: FBA to estimate intracellular fluxes also provides flux values that can be used as a starting point for\nL35@P0: rational engineering of C. reinhardtii. From these initial estimates, it is clear that aerobic\nL36@P0: heterotrophic growth on acetate has a low yield on carbon, while mixotrophically and\nL37@P0: autotrophically grown cells are significantly more carbon efficient.\nL38@P0: Published: 7 January 2009\nL39@P0: BMC Systems Biology 2009, 3:4 doi:10.1186/1752-0509-3-4\nL40@P0: Received: 12 August 2008\nL41@P0: Accepted: 7 January 2009\nL42@P0: This article is available from: http://www.biomedcentral.com/1752-0509/3/4\nL43@P0: © 2009 Boyle and Morgan; licensee BioMed Central Ltd.\nL44@P0: This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0),\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlux balance analysis of primary metabolism in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii | BMC Systems Biology | Springer Nature Link (https://bmcsystbiol.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4 ; rewritten summary of https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4)\nciteturn72view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn70view0\",\"pattern\":\"TAP\"}); Redirected to URL: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4; Total lines: 579\nL214: Unlike heterotrophic organisms that utilize the same substrate as the source of both carbon and energy, photoautotrophic organisms require two substrates, one for energy (light) and one for carbon (carbon dioxide). Due to the input of two substrates, FBA simulations can be run in either light or carbon limitation conditions. To simulate carbon limitation, the model is allowed unlimited light, which calculates an optimal biomass flux.\nL215: No fermentation products were detected in the media during growth (see maximum uptake rates discussion) therefore, in the absence of carbon overflow products, the yield of biomass is fixed because the only outlet for carbon is biomass. For photoautotrophic metabolism, a more meaningful result is to find the flux distribution that maximizes biomass while minimizing energy usage. Therefore the optimization is done in two steps.\nL216: The first step is to maximize biomass with no constraint on light and the second is to fix the biomass and minimize light. Flux distributions for the heterotrophic case are the result of a one-step optimization to maximize biomass.\nL217: #### Culture conditions\nL218: Chlamydomonas reinhardtii strain CC-400 cw15 mt+ was acquired from the Chlamydomonas Genetics Center. Cells were cultivated at 25°C in 250 ml flasks with a working volume of 50 ml and an agitation rate of 200 RPM. Heterotrophic and mixotrophic cells were grown in TAP media [【83†43】] and autotrophic cells were grown in similar media without addition of acetic acid. Mixotrophic and autotrophic cultures were grown under constant illumination at an average fluence rate of 65 μE/m^{2}/s.\nL219: All cells were grown in the presence of atmospheric carbon dioxide levels. Growth was monitored spectrophotometrically by measuring absorbance at 750 nm.\nL220: #### Maximum uptake rates\nL221: In order to add constraints on nutrient and light uptake to the model, additional experimental measurements were taken. Maximum growth rates were measured for all three growth conditions from three separate experiments. The results are shown in Table 【84†7】. For autotrophic growth, the maximum carbon dioxide uptake rate was calculated to be 2.04 mmol/g biomass/hr. The maximum solar light flux was set to be 2100 μE/m^{2}/s [【85†44】].\nL222: For heterotrophic growth, the maximum acetate uptake rate was measured using high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) coupled to an refractive index detector (RID) detector and was found to be 12.06 mmol/g biomass/hr. The same HPLC method was used to look for fermentation products, but none were detected and therefore they were not included in the model.\nL223: The biomass growth yield on acetate was measured in exponentially growing cells to be 3.12 ± 0.23 kg biomass/100 moles acetate from three independent experiments.\nL224: Table 7 Specific growth rates\nL225: #### Estimation of cell surface area\nL226: In order to convert the calculated total photons from the model to a flux (μE/m^{2}/s), the surface area per kilogram biomass must be calculated. Based on experimental measurements, the dry weight of a typical C. reinhardtii was determined to be 0.2 pg. The length and width of cell were assumed to be 10 μm and 3 μm respectively [【83†43】] based on literature values. The geometry of the cell was assumed to be a prolate spheroid.\nL227: The surface area per kilogram biomass was then calculated to be 389 m^{2}kg^{-1}.\nL228: #### Maintenance requirements\nL229: Growth associated and non-growth associated maintenance requirements were also included in the model. Growth associated energy is included to account for partially unknown energy requirements for transport, biosynthesis and polymerization [【82†42】] while non-growth associated accounts for cellular maintenance operations such as DNA repair, cell wall maintenance, and pH control.\nL230: Growth associated maintenance was found to be 29.89 mmol ATP/g biomass by fitting the model to the experimentally determined biomass yield by changing the ATP requirement. This value falls into the range of published values for growth associated maintenance values [【22†2】–【23†7】]. Autotrophic and mixotrophic maintenance requirements were assumed to be the same. Non-growth associated maintenance requirements range from 0.36 mmol ATP/g DW hr for Lactobacillus plantarum to 7.60 mmol ATP/g DW for E. coli.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlux balance analysis of primary metabolism in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii | BMC Systems Biology | Springer Nature Link (https://bmcsystbiol.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4 ; rewritten summary of https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4)\nciteturn72view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn70view0\",\"pattern\":\"Zinc\"}); Redirected to URL: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4; Total lines: 579\nNo matching text found for \"Zinc\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFlux balance analysis of primary metabolism in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii | BMC Systems Biology | Springer Nature Link (https://bmcsystbiol.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4 ; rewritten summary of https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4)\nciteturn72view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn70view0\",\"pattern\":\"Additional\"}); Redirected to URL: https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4; Total lines: 579\nL75: There are several widely accepted methods for modelling metabolism, ranging from highly detailed kinetic models to less complex stoichiometric models. One of the more increasingly used methods is flux balance analysis (FBA), which has the ability to predict fluxes using linear programming with the knowledge of reaction stoichiometry, biomass composition and additional constraints, such as limits on uptake/excretion rates and thermodynamic constraints.\nL76: FBA has been used for a number of model organisms [【22†2】–【23†7】] to predict fluxes and viability of knockouts. FBA can also be used for rational strain design, both to predict theoretical yields and to identify bottlenecks or sinks in metabolism that need to be altered to achieve the theoretical yield [【24†8】, 【25†9】].\nL99: Predicting the localization of an enzyme is only possible when the amino acid sequence is known, therefore for the few enzymes that were not in the database, the localization was assumed based on where the preceding and following metabolic steps were located. Metabolites were also assigned to compartments based on which compartment they were in while participating in enzymatic reactions; these are differentiated by a small m (mitochondria) or c (chloroplast) following the metabolite abbreviation.\nL100: Metabolites without a subscript, by default, are located in the cytosol [see additional file 【43†1】 for a complete list of metabolites]. Metabolites were allowed to move between compartments either through known transporters and shuttle systems or through inferred reactions (or passive diffusion) based on the need for metabolites in certain compartments.\nL101: Figure 2\nL102: \nL103: 【44†Image: Figure 2†media.springernature.com】\nL104: \nL105: 【45†Full size image】\nL106: \nL107: Reconstructed metabolic network of C. reinhardtii. Based on predicted target peptide sequences, the following localization of pathways was determined. Chloroplast: fatty acid synthesis, amino acid synthesis, nucleotide synthesis, starch synthesis and chlorophyll synthesis. Mitochondria: TCA cycle, amino acid synthesis. Cytosol: glycolysis, amino acid synthesis and fatty acid synthesis.\nL108: ### Biomass formation equation\nL109: The macromolecular composition of cells from each growth condition was measured as described in the methods section. The DNA and RNA content were assumed constant across all growth conditions. The final cellular dry weight composition of each growth condition is given in Table 【46†2】. The elemental composition was also measured (Table 【47†3】) and compared to the values calculated from the measured cellular composition. The final balances varied from the measured elemental composition by 0.1% to 6%.\nL134: Figure 4\nL135: \nL136: 【59†Image: Figure 4†media.springernature.com】\nL137: \nL138: 【60†Full size image】\nL139: \nL140: Heterotrophic central metabolism flux map. The thickness of the arrows has been normalized to the total acetate uptake of 100 moles. The green compartment represents the chloroplast and the orange compartment is the mitochondria.\nL141: C. reinhardtii is also capable of mixotrophic growth, utilizing acetate, light and carbon dioxide for growth. Mixotrophic growth was simulated by using heterotrophic growth as the base case and allowing the uptake of carbon dioxide and light for energy. The free uptake of carbon dioxide was allowed, however to limit the biomass formed, an additional constraint on the absorbed light was added.\nL142: The total absorbed light flux was fixed over a range of 0 to 2 μE/m^{2}/s in a step-wise fashion and at each light flux, the optimal flux distribution was calculated. Fluxes that changed significantly over the range of absorbed light are plotted in Figure 【61†5】. From this graph it is evident there are two distinct regions of growth. The first resembles heterotrophic growth in which carbon fixation does not occur and the cell is producing CO_{2}.\nL162: Flux estimates for autotrophic growth were compared to estimated fluxes for the cyanobacteria, Synechocystis sp PCC 6803 [【25†9】]. One major difference is the flux through the cyclic and non-cyclic ETCs (Table 【66†6】). Although both organisms utilize approximately the same amount of energy to produce each kilogram of biomass, the flux through the ETCs are split quite differently. Synechocystis has a much higher flux through the non-cyclic ETC than C.\nL163: reinhardtii which is due mainly to the compartmentation in the model; due to a lack of a direct NAD(P)H transporter in C. reinhardtii, the flux through the non-cyclic ETC is constrained to match the need for NADPH in the chloroplast. Any additional NAD(P)H is indirectly transported via shuttles, but these shuttles are also constrained by mass balances and steady state assumptions.\nL164: In contrast, since Synechocystis is prokaryotic and unicellular, it can use the non-cyclic ETC to produce all the NADPH needed in the cell. Therefore, to make up for the energetic difference of having a lower flux through the non-cyclic ETC, C. reinhardtii must have a larger flux through the cyclic ETC. Cellular compartmentation also comes into play in the total moles of oxygen produced.\nL181: Fatty acid synthesis reactions were added to represent the synthesis of hexadecanoic and octadecanoic acids as well as their corresponding unsaturated fatty acids (16:1, 16:2, 16:3, 16:4, 18:1, 18:2, 18:3, 18:4), which represent the majority of fatty acids present in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii [【76†37】].\nL182: The synthesis of the major classes of lipids (MGDG, DGDG, SQDG, PG, PI, DGTS, and PE) were included based on average lipid composition for each head group and the localization of each lipid was based on the distribution of C_{16} and C_{18} in the C1 and C2 position on the head group [【76†37】]. Although detailed lipid synthesis reactions were included in the network reconstruction, lipids were lumped into a single representative lipid to simplify the FBA simulations.\nL183: An 'average' lipid made up of unsaturated C18 fatty acids and a glycerol head group was assumed based on the largest percentage of lipids being C18 as reported by Janero and Barrnett [【77†38】] and is shown in reaction 179 in additional file 【78†2】.\nL184: Despite large efforts to fully annotate genome databases, not all enzymatic functions are listed and therefore gaps exist in the pathways. Gaps in the network were first addressed by searching pathway databases for the missing enzymes and corresponding genes in other organisms whose genome is sequenced (A. thaliana, E. coli, and S. cerevisiae). The amino acid sequence of these known genes were then blasted against the C.\nL185: reinhardtii database; in most cases, this resulted in a hit which had already been annotated but not linked to the KEGG portion of the genome database. There were a few genes that resulted in hits to proteins that were either listed as having a different function or were not annotated at all (see results). A few enzymes resulted in no hits in the database, but were assumed to be present in order to have a complete network.\nL186: ### Phosphorylation\nL187: Accurate reconstruction of a metabolic network requires the inclusion of reactions for cellular energy production. Two main sources of energy in algae are photophosphorylation and oxidative phosphorylation. Photophosphorylation is the process by which light is converted into energy (ATP) and reducing power (NADPH) via two electron transport chains (ETC).\nL188: The cyclic ETC is made up of several membrane-bound and membrane associated proteins that are coupled with the light harvesting complex, photosystem I (PSI) which pumps protons across the chloroplast membrane. The non-cyclic ETC uses both light harvesting complexes (photosystem I and photosystem II) to produce NADPH and pump protons across the plastidic membrane. This process has been modelled previously in Synechocystis by Shastri and Morgan (2005) as two non-interacting reactions as shown below:\nL201: Oxidative phosphorylation occurs via the mitochondrial ETC, which is made up of 4 complexes (I, II, III, and IV) that control the flow of electrons from NADH to reduce oxygen to water. The ETC is also coupled to an ATP synthase which produces ATP from ADP by pumping protons across the mitochondria membrane. Although the exact H^{+}/ATP ratio for the mitochondria ATP synthase has not yet been agreed upon, it is assumed to be between 3 and 4 [【79†39】–【80†41】].\nL203: $${\\text{3 H}}_{\\text{m}}^{+}+{\\text{Pi}}_{\\text{m}}+{\\text{ADP}}_{\\text{m}}\\to {\\text{ATP}}_{\\text{m}}$$\nL204: \nL205: (4)\nL206: #### Linear programming formulation\nL207: The reconstructed metabolic network provided the information necessary to develop a stoichiometric model [【82†42】]. A stoichiometric model takes the form S·v = 0, where S is the stoichiometric matrix and v is a vector of fluxes. The stoichiometric matrix and flux vector is constructed by writing a steady-state mass balance on each intracellular metabolite in each compartment, this set of mass balances is then converted to matrix form.\nL208: Reversible reactions are separated into one forward and one reverse reaction in order to constrain all fluxes to be positive, therefore minimizing solution time. An additional constraint was added to only allow one direction of a reversible reaction to be active by introducing a binary variable [【25†9】]. Since the resulting model was underdetermined, linear programming was used to solve for optimal fluxes.\nL209: $$\\begin{array}{l}\\text{Maximize biomass subject to:}\\\\ {\\displaystyle \\sum _{j}{\\text{s}}_{\\text{ij}}{\\upsilon }_{j}=0\\text{ for every i}\\in {\\text{M}}_{\\text{i}}}\\\\ {\\displaystyle \\sum _{j}{\\text{s}}_{\\text{ij}}{\\upsilon }_{j}\\le 0\\text{ for every i}\\in {\\text{M}}_{\\text{r}}}\\\\ {\\displaystyle \\sum _{j}{\\text{s}}_{\\text{ij}}{\\upsilon }_{j}\\ge 0\\text{ for every i}\\in {\\text{M}}_{\\text{p}}}\\\\ {\\upsilon }_{j}\\ge 0\\end{array}$$\nL210: where s_{ij} is the stoichiometric coefficient of the i^{th} metabolite in the j^{th} reaction, v_{j} is the flux of the j^{th} reaction, M_{i} is the set of intracellular metabolites, M_{r} is the set of reactants other than substrate, and M_{p} is the set of products excreted.\nL211: \nL212: A mixed integer linear program was formulated in the GAMS environment (GAMS Development Corporation, Washington, DC) and the optimum solution was found using the ILOG CPLEX 8.100 solver (ILOG, Inc. Mountain View, CA).\nL213: #### Optimization\nL214: Unlike heterotrophic organisms that utilize the same substrate as the source of both carbon and energy, photoautotrophic organisms require two substrates, one for energy (light) and one for carbon (carbon dioxide). Due to the input of two substrates, FBA simulations can be run in either light or carbon limitation conditions. To simulate carbon limitation, the model is allowed unlimited light, which calculates an optimal biomass flux.\nL215: No fermentation products were detected in the media during growth (see maximum uptake rates discussion) therefore, in the absence of carbon overflow products, the yield of biomass is fixed because the only outlet for carbon is biomass. For photoautotrophic metabolism, a more meaningful result is to find the flux distribution that maximizes biomass while minimizing energy usage. Therefore the optimization is done in two steps.\nL216: The first step is to maximize biomass with no constraint on light and the second is to fix the biomass and minimize light. Flux distributions for the heterotrophic case are the result of a one-step optimization to maximize biomass.\nL218: Chlamydomonas reinhardtii strain CC-400 cw15 mt+ was acquired from the Chlamydomonas Genetics Center. Cells were cultivated at 25°C in 250 ml flasks with a working volume of 50 ml and an agitation rate of 200 RPM. Heterotrophic and mixotrophic cells were grown in TAP media [【83†43】] and autotrophic cells were grown in similar media without addition of acetic acid. Mixotrophic and autotrophic cultures were grown under constant illumination at an average fluence rate of 65 μE/m^{2}/s.\nL219: All cells were grown in the presence of atmospheric carbon dioxide levels. Growth was monitored spectrophotometrically by measuring absorbance at 750 nm.\nL220: #### Maximum uptake rates\nL221: In order to add constraints on nutrient and light uptake to the model, additional experimental measurements were taken. Maximum growth rates were measured for all three growth conditions from three separate experiments. The results are shown in Table 【84†7】. For autotrophic growth, the maximum carbon dioxide uptake rate was calculated to be 2.04 mmol/g biomass/hr. The maximum solar light flux was set to be 2100 μE/m^{2}/s [【85†44】].\nL222: For heterotrophic growth, the maximum acetate uptake rate was measured using high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) coupled to an refractive index detector (RID) detector and was found to be 12.06 mmol/g biomass/hr. The same HPLC method was used to look for fermentation products, but none were detected and therefore they were not included in the model.\nL223: The biomass growth yield on acetate was measured in exponentially growing cells to be 3.12 ± 0.23 kg biomass/100 moles acetate from three independent experiments.\nL224: Table 7 Specific growth rates\nL225: #### Estimation of cell surface area\nL226: In order to convert the calculated total photons from the model to a flux (μE/m^{2}/s), the surface area per kilogram biomass must be calculated. Based on experimental measurements, the dry weight of a typical C. reinhardtii was determined to be 0.2 pg. The length and width of cell were assumed to be 10 μm and 3 μm respectively [【83†43】] based on literature values. The geometry of the cell was assumed to be a prolate spheroid.\nL233: The biomass composition was determined separately for each of the three growth regimes: autotrophic, mixotrophic and heterotrophic growth. Lipids were measured using the chloroform-methanol extraction method of Ishida et al. [【87†45】]. The resulting water layer and pellet were then dried and resuspended in 0.2 N NaOH and diluted by a factor of 5. This solution was then assayed for protein content with the Pierce BCA protein assay kit (Pierce Biotechnology, Inc. Rockford, IL).\nL234: The amino acid composition (Additional file 【88†3】) was estimated from Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) analysis of hydrolyzed protein (data not shown). Chlorophyll a and b were measured [【83†43】] and subtracted from the total lipid measurement. DNA and RNA were assumed to be constant for all growth conditions; the DNA content was determined by Chiang et al[【89†46】] to be 1.23 × 10^{-7} μg per cell and the RNA content was assumed to be 28-fold higher than the DNA content [【90†47】].\nL235: The GC content of DNA was measured to be 62.1% [【83†43】] and the same GC content was assumed for RNA. Carbohydrate composition was calculated as the balance of the fraction dry weight. The elemental composition of lyophilized cells was also determined. In all cases except elemental composition, experiments were done in triplicate.\nL437:   44. Metting FB, Smith J, Amthor J, Izaurralde RC: Science Needs and New Technology for Increasing Soil Carbon Sequestration. Climatic Change. 2001, 51 (1): 11-34. 10.1023/A:1017509224801.\nL438: \nL439:   45. Ishida T, Hasegawa N, Hayashi NR, Peerapornpisal Y, Ishii M, Igarashi Y, Kodama T: Growth Characteristics and Dense Culture of a Thermophilic Cyanobacterium, Chroococcidiopsis sp. Strain TS-821. Journal of Fermentation and Bioengineering. 1997, 83 (6): 571-576. 10.1016/S0922-338X(97)81139-7.\nL440:   46. Chiang K-S, Sueoka N: Replication of Chloroplast DNA in Chlamydomonas reinhardi during Vegetative Cell Cycle: Its Mode and Regulation. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA. 1967, 57 (5): 1506-1513. 10.1073/pnas.57.5.1506\nL441: \nL442:   47. Valle O, Lien T, Knutsen G: Fluorometric determination of DNA and RNA in Chlamydomonas using ethidium bromide. Journal of Biochemical and Biophysical Methods. 1981, 4: 271-277. 10.1016/0165-022X(81)90067-1.\nL443: ## Acknowledgements\nL444: \nL445: The authors would like to thank Avantika Shastri for her assistance in analyzing amino acid compositions and valuable discussion. This material is based upon work supported under a National Science Foundation Graduate Research Fellowship awarded to NRB and the NSF CAREER award to JAM (BES-0348458).\nL446: \nL447: ## Author information\nL448: ### Authors and Affiliations\nL449: \nL450:   1. School of Chemical Engineering, Purdue University, 480 Stadium Mall Drive, West Lafayette, Indiana, 47907, USA\nL451: \nL452: Nanette R Boyle & John A Morgan\nL453: \nL454: ### Corresponding author\nL455: \nL456: Correspondence to John A Morgan (jamorgan@purdue.edu).\nL457: \nL458: ## Additional information\nL459: ### Authors' contributions\nL460: \nL461: NRB carried out all aspects of the work and drafted the manuscript. JAM conceived the study and participated in the design of the study and revisions of the manuscript. All authors read and approve the final manuscript.\nL462: \nL463: ## Electronic supplementary material\nL464: ### 【91†12918_2008_272_MOESM1_ESM.xls (download XLS )†static-content.springer.com】\nL465: \nL466: Additional file 1: Metabolite list. Comprehensive list of metabolites and abbreviations used in the metabolic reconstruction and FBA model for C. reinhardtii. (XLS 66 KB)\nL467: \nL468: ### 【92†12918_2008_272_MOESM2_ESM.xls (download XLS )†static-content.springer.com】\nL469: \nL470: Additional file 2: Reaction list. Comprehensive list of reactions used in the metabolic reconstruction and FBA model for C. reinhardtii. (XLS 128 KB)\nL471: ### 【93†12918_2008_272_MOESM3_ESM.xls (download XLS )†static-content.springer.com】\nL472: \nL473: Additional file 3: Amino acid compositions. Amino acid compositions for each growth condition (auto-, hetero-, mixo-). (XLS 19 KB)\nL474: \nL475: ## Authors’ original submitted files for images\nL476: \nL477: Below are the links to the authors’ original submitted files for images.\nL478: \nL479: ### 【94†Authors’ original file for figure 1 (download PDF )†static-content.springer.com】\nL480: ### 【95†Authors’ original file for figure 2 (download PDF )†static-content.springer.com】\nL481: \nL482: ### 【96†Authors’ original file for figure 3 (download PDF )†static-content.springer.com】\nL483: \nL484: ### 【97†Authors’ original file for figure 4 (download PDF )†static-content.springer.com】\nL485: \nL486: ### 【98†Authors’ original file for figure 5 (download PDF )†static-content.springer.com】\nL487: ## Rights and permissions\nL488: \nL489: This article is published under license to BioMed Central Ltd. This is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License (【99†http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0†creativecommons.org】), which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.\nL490: \nL491: 【100†Reprints and permissions†s100.copyright.com】\nL492: \nL493: ## About this article\nL494: ### Cite this article\nL495: \nL496: Boyle, N.R., Morgan, J.A. Flux balance analysis of primary metabolism in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii. BMC Syst Biol 3, 4 (2009). https://doi.org/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4\nL497: \nL498:   * Received: 12 August 2008\nL499: \nL500:   * Accepted: 07 January 2009\nL501: \nL502:   * Published: 07 January 2009\nL503: \nL504:   * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1186/1752-0509-3-4\nL505: ### Share this article\nL506: \nL507: Anyone you share the following link with will be able to read this content:\nL508: \nL509: Sorry, a shareable link is not currently available for this article.\nL510: \nL511: Provided by the Springer Nature Share